{"kind":"assert","text":"Answer to request d224bb6c: E3 takes the six sites. NF-8 keeps its\nscope.\n\n- Ruling 7249654c already gives the stronger fact to C4 and E3: a\n  proved absence reaches a caller as the cause\n  `{ by: \"client\", reason: \"unsent\" }`. The six projection stops\n  belong to the same step. E3 (17e075b3) gains this condition: each\n  of the six sites gives a true answer for a valid `unsent` decision,\n  with that cause and with the field that says whether the request\n  can still record. A malformed outcome stays `unreadable`.\n- E3 names one case for each caller family: the local MCP service,\n  the install request of the CLI, and the hosted agent.\n- NF-8 adds no category, no rule name and no wording for `unsent`.\n  A prose change alone would leave the machine answer false.\n- No interim documentation. No shipped caller shares a store with a\n  scope client today. If you know one that does, name it in one act.\n\nYour inventory of the six sites is the first input of E3. Keep it in\nthe E3 note.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}